codex
**Findings**
- `high` [services/approvals.py:43](/Users/anishguntreddi/Documents/hackathon/apps/api/captureos/services/approvals.py:43), [api/filings.py:283](/Users/anishguntreddi/Documents/hackathon/apps/api/captureos/api/filings.py:283): recommendation approval does not require a `Recommendation` row. If `rec is None`, the code still sets `filing.status = "approved"`, and `/build-package` keys only off that status. An editor can approve a nonexistent recommendation and unlock package build.

- `high` [services/approvals.py:53](/Users/anishguntreddi/Documents/hackathon/apps/api/captureos/services/approvals.py:53): package approval has no filing-state precondition. If a validated latest package exists, `target="package"` can move the filing to `ready` from the wrong state, including after recommendation rejection (`recommended`) or without first being in `package_review`. That re-enables export on stale docs.

- `high` [services/packaging.py:282](/Users/anishguntreddi/Documents/hackathon/apps/api/captureos/services/packaging.py:282), [services/approvals.py:78](/Users/anishguntreddi/Documents/hackathon/apps/api/captureos/services/approvals.py:78): `validate_citations()` only checks that `citations[]` entries resolve to any known `evidence_item_id` or `source_id`, plus “non-empty” for claim-bearing docs. It does not verify that every claim/marker in `content_md` is cited, and it accepts source-only citations. A narrative/compliance doc with unsourced prose plus one valid citation can reach `citation_validated=True`, and the package gate treats that as sufficient for `ready`.

- `medium` [api/filings.py:280](/Users/anishguntreddi/Documents/hackathon/apps/api/captureos/api/filings.py:280), [workflows/dispatch.py:16](/Users/anishguntreddi/Documents/hackathon/apps/api/captureos/workflows/dispatch.py:16), [workflows/runner.py:16](/Users/anishguntreddi/Documents/hackathon/apps/api/captureos/workflows/runner.py:16), [services/packaging.py:187](/Users/anishguntreddi/Documents/hackathon/apps/api/captureos/services/packaging.py:187): rebuild does not revoke `ready` at request time. `/build-package` only enqueues background work; the filing stays `ready` until the worker transaction later commits `packaging/package_review`. The previously approved package remains exportable during that window.

